n red. Engineer Pip Solano was already moving, cable in teeth, splicing by feel. 'Talk to me,' Commander Ash Korv said, and Engineer Pip Solano talked — numbers, jokes, anything to keep the fear small."}, {'n': 4, 'title': "The Traitor's Price", 'text': "Commander Ash Korv let them live, confined to quarters. 'the Void Syndicate trades in hostages,' Commander Ash Korv told the crew. 'We trade in something better.' It was a gamble; the crew's eyes said they knew it.\n\nThe traitor showed themselves at the third waypoint: the signal officer, paid in promises by the Void Syndicate. They seized an alien engine core and ran for the escape pod with the Void Syndicate's ships closing in.\n\nEngineer Pip Solano caught them at the airlock. There was no fight — only the traitor crying that the Void Syndicate held their family. Commander Ash Korv understood, and hated understanding. Mercy, out here, was a luxury with teeth."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Grid Awakens', 'text': "The flagship came on anyway, and Commander Ash Korv met it ship to ship above the burning sky. It was the Void Syndicate themselves on the viewscreen, offering one last deal. Commander Ash Korv answered with the engines at full burn.\n\nThe duel was legend before it ended: two ships in the fire-light, neither yielding, until Commander Ash Korv did the impossible — dove through the grid's own gate, which an alien engine core held open, and watched the Void Syndicate break against the shield they could not see.\n\nSilence, then. The grid humming. the Cygnus Expanse safe below. Engineer Pip Solano put a hand on Commander Ash Korv's shoulder and said what everyone was thinking: 'We live.'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Home Signal', 'text': "They landed at the Cygnus Expanse to crowds and bells. The old voice from the starlog had belonged to the grid's last keeper, dead a hundred years; an alien engine core was their final message. Commander Ash Korv laid it in the memorial with honors.\n\nAnd when the next distress call came — as it always does, out in the drift — the engines were already warm. There are always more skies. There is always the Cygnus Expanse, somewhere, needing its heroes.\n\nEngineer Pip Solano was offered an admiralty and laughed in the committee's face. 'I have a ship,' they said. It was the highest compliment the crew had ever heard."}]}

